Unverified external Unity package bundled in releases
Link: https://chatgpt.com/codex/cloud/security/findings/fa08ed3c46e4819185948dbcba8e4fa0?sev=critical%2Chigh
Criticality: high (attack path: high)
Status: new

# Metadata
Repo: hatolife/ClipForVRChat
Commit: 16bfb40
Author: poppo@hato.life
Created: 2026/7/8 1:45:41
Assignee: Unassigned
Signals: Security, Validated, Patch generated, Attack-path

# Summary
Introduced: the release workflow accepts mutable external release content as a trusted release component without pinning or verifying its expected digest/signature before redistribution.
This commit changes the release process from packaging repository-controlled AvatarBeacon source to downloading a prebuilt .unitypackage at release time. The workflow only checks that the downloaded file exists and is non-empty, then calculates and records the hash after download. It never compares the package to a committed expected SHA256 or verifies an upstream signature/attestation. The package is then copied into both user-facing release zips. Because the PGP signature step signs only the standalone ClipForVRChat.exe, not the zip contents or the Unity package, users who verify the exe signature could still receive an attacker-controlled AvatarBeacon package inside an official release zip if the external AvatarBeacon release asset, tag, repository account, or GitHub-hosted asset is compromised/replaced before a ClipForVRChat release build. Unity packages may contain scripts that execute in the Unity editor when imported, so this can become arbitrary code execution in the user's Unity environment after expected user interaction.

# Validation
## Rubric
- [x] Confirm the affected commit changed the release process to use an external AvatarBeacon `.unitypackage` instead of repository-controlled source packaging.
- [x] Confirm the workflow pins only URL/name and does not define or compare a committed expected SHA256, signature, or attestation for the AvatarBeacon package.
- [x] Confirm the download step validates only existence/non-empty size and computes SHA256 after download rather than enforcing a trusted expected value.
- [x] Confirm the downloaded package is copied into both user-facing release package directories/zips.
- [x] Confirm the signing/release process authenticates only the standalone executable and not the zips or bundled Unity package.
## Report
Validated the finding as a CI/release supply-chain trust flaw, not a runtime memory-safety crash. Crash/Valgrind/debugger validation is not applicable because the affected component is `.github/workflows/release.yml`; valgrind and gdb were also not installed in the container. Evidence: `.github/workflows/release.yml:22-23` defines `AVATARBEACON_PACKAGE_URL` and `AVATARBEACON_PACKAGE_NAME` but no expected digest/signature variable. `.github/workflows/release.yml:167-188` downloads the external asset with `Invoke-WebRequest`, checks only `Test-Path` and `$item.Length -le 0`, then computes `package_sha256` after download. A targeted grep for AvatarBeacon expected digest/signature controls returned only post-download hash metadata: line 277 `AVATARBEACON_PACKAGE_SHA256: ${{ steps.avatarbeacon.outputs.package_sha256 }}` and line 298 `packageSha256 = $env:AVATARBEACON_PACKAGE_SHA256`; no pinned expected SHA/signature/attestation check was found. `.github/workflows/release.yml:210` and `:219` copy the downloaded package into both `dist/ClipForVRChat/` and `dist/ClipForVRChat-user-package/`. `.github/workflows/release.yml:222-243` signs and verifies only `dist/${ASSET_PREFIX}-windows-amd64.exe`, while `.github/workflows/release.yml:254` and `:258` zip directories that contain the Unity package. `.github/workflows/release.yml:529-532` uploads the two zips and the exe `.asc`, but no zip/package signature. The commit diff confirms this was introduced by adding the external download and removing repository-generated `AvatarBeacon-*-source.zip` packaging. I also ran a bounded local PoC that mirrors the relevant workflow behavior: it created an attacker-controlled non-empty `AvatarBeacon_v0.0.1.unitypackage`, accepted it with only a post-download SHA256, copied it into both release zip trees, signed only the executable, and verified that the exe signature remains valid while the unsigned package is present in the zip. PoC output included: `download-step accepted AvatarBeacon_v0.0.1.unitypackage with post-download sha256=d42cfe6c9dedcd9f8a7a84a2ec8179e86c99c14133be43980eb1342f5e30e307`, both zips listing `AvatarBeacon_v0.0.1.unitypackage`, embedded payload `ATTACKER-CONTROLLED UNITYPACKAGE: would contain Unity Editor scripts/assets`, and GPG `Good signature from "CFVRC PoC <poc@example.invalid>"` for the exe only. This demonstrates that mutable upstream release content can become part of official release zips without an authenticated expected value.

